Three independent refuters on three repositories reported the row-level
precision denominator as uncheckable against the source. They were right, and
the cause is DISCLOSURE, not arithmetic.

THE RULE, established by live repro on all three named repositories. The count
is the number of (SOURCE CALL SITE, SINK CALL SITE) pairs where the sink site is
reachable from the source site's caller. The record emitted it beside
source_primitives / sink_primitives -- DISTINCT NAMES -- and nothing about site
multiplicity, so two rows with identical primitives legitimately carried
different counts with nothing to explain the difference: shellcheck showed three
rows at 1x1 -> 1 and a fourth at 1x1 -> 2. The 2 is correct -- `striptests`
reads env ONCE and reaches `redirect.>` at TWO sites, the file node and
`sponge`, which it reaches.

Findings now carry sink_call_sites as (caller, callee) PAIRS, unioned across a
collapsed group, so the multiplier is bounded by
len(source_primitives) * len(sink_call_sites).

THE FIRST CUT OF THIS FIX WAS WRONG AND THE LIVE RE-RUN CAUGHT IT. Recording
the sink CALLER alone collapsed kamaraflow's four sinks -- open, file.write,
os.makedirs, shutil.copyfile, all called from one function -- to a single site,
leaving collapsed_flow_count=8 against a bound of 2. Both sides multiply, and
every unit test written for the shellcheck shape passed either way. That case is
now pinned as its own test. MEASURED AFTER THE CORRECTION: 18 of 18 evidence
rows across the three repositories reconcile, ALL EXACTLY (kamaraflow 8 = 2x4,
ArkLib 6 = 1x6, shellcheck 2 = 1x2).

NO DOUBLE-COUNTING FOUND, and the item's "inflated in at least one direction" is
not carried forward. source_callers is built one entry per EDGE and edges are
deduplicated on (src, dst, edge_type) (INV-vukiv), so a site cannot enter twice.
The refuters' counts were low because they counted primitive OCCURRENCES in
source text while the tool counts reachable SITE PAIRS.

NOT FIXED HERE, and it is the larger point. For analysis_method=structural the
pair test is pure call-graph reachability with no check that a value crosses --
which is what ADR-0017 defines structural to be. A rate computed over this count
is therefore a rate over REACHABILITY PAIRS, not over value-flow claims, and it
grows with how much a file reaches rather than with anything about taint
(kamaraflow's source is a FILE node). That is an independent, mechanical reason
0006 was right to make SITUATION canonical.

ALSO FIXES A WI-TUBIJ REGRESSION ALREADY ON dev.
test_bash_env_read_source.py::test_bash_now_carries_both_halves froze the exact
list of bash taint-source rows; WI-tubij's clock additions ($EPOCHREALTIME,
$EPOCHSECONDS, $SECONDS) broke it while changing nothing the test is about, and
it went red on dev because that PR's hand-extended manifest did not select this
file. The assertion now tests the SPLIT its own docstring claims -- both labels
derive, env.environ is the host_secret half, every host_description source stays
in the shell module -- instead of an inventory that decays on the next correct
catalogue edit.

